From 494c5598f16d2086cc2f30f185a64bd4d4b151aa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noel Blatter <noel.blatter@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 11:25:35 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Fast path covers parameter links with other functions than
 sqrt

A setup whose link used any other function than jnp.sqrt fell back to the original
JAX implementation, with its full tracing and compilation. Two additions:

- LINK_RULES has the other elementary functions of jax.numpy (square, exp, log,
  log10, sin, cos, tan, tanh, arctan, negative, reciprocal, abs, radians, degrees),
  each with its derivative.
- A link written as a plain formula (a lambda or def without branches that uses
  arithmetic and jnp functions) is evaluated on a dual number: a copy of the function
  with jnp bound to the NumPy shim of the component functions gives the value and the
  derivative together (_formula_rule). Anything that cannot be evaluated that way
  (comparisons, jnp.where, jit objects, other libraries) has no rule, and the setup
  keeps using the JAX implementation as before.

The shim learns log, log10, tan, tanh, arctan, square, negative, reciprocal and
degrees for dual numbers; for plain numbers it computes what it did.

runs/noel/test_link_functions.py (branch noel): aLIGO with amplitude modulation, the
link function replaced by 22 covered functions and formulas, against the dense JAX
reference: log sensitivity within 1.1e-8, outputs 9.8e-12, gradient 1.6e-11 (the laser
power is a design variable, so the gradient runs through the link); five uncovered
functions are reported as unsupported. Both benchmarks pass, their arrays are
bit-identical to main.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
